      "writtenOn": "2025-11-04T01:19:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Pointer manipulation here may bypass C++ stdlib hardening - I would suggest to replace this with `operator[]` instead:\n\n```suggestion\n        const uint8_t* srcRow \u003d \u0026fDecodingState-\u003efRustDecodedData[y * srcRowBytes];\n```\n\nEven better, we probably should use `span`s instead of raw pointers.  `SkSpan` is a bit limited compared to `std::span` (or to Chromium\u0027s `base::span`), but it still seems beneficial.  So maybe (hand-waving) something like this:\n\n```\n    const SkSpan\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e srcImage \u003d fDecodingState-\u003efRustDecodedData;\n    for (int y \u003d 0; y \u003c height; ++y) {\n        const SkSpan\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e srcRow \u003d srcImage.first(srcRowBytes);\n        // ...\n        \n        srcImage \u003d srcImage.subspan(srcRowBytes);\n    }\n```\n\n(Using spans will also side-step the question if the multiplication here is safe from integer overflow.)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 183,
        "startChar": 32,
        "endLine": 183,
        "endChar": 89
      },
      "revId": "cc6e7f648b2c05b805a99333be3bfdfea2dcc491",
